From 0e2dea0d89bb61687f604f566d33572feaa7e28a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 11:28:46 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 48/48] ggml-cuda: report command timing and trace spans

skippy_set_gpu_command_timing_callback returned UNSUPPORTED on CUDA, so a
CUDA trace had no GPU time in it: no execute windows, no idle gaps, nothing
to separate the GPU running from the host preparing the next step. That is
the gap behind every CUDA time-to-first-token question.

The CUDA registry now exposes the three timing procs through the shared
ggml-backend-timing state, so Skippy's existing forwarding and the Rust
gpu_commands telemetry pick CUDA up unchanged: spans arrive as
cuda.command_buffer with encode, queue and execute children, and
cuda.idle where the GPU waited.

A record is one graph compute. Two events on the context's main stream
bracket it, recorded outside any CUDA graph capture, so eager, captured and
replayed computes are timed alike; the encode window is the host's
submission, capture and instantiation included. Event times reach the Unix
clock through an anchor event the host waited for, renewed once a second at
a synchronize when nothing pending refers to it. Records are reported from
the next compute or synchronize once their end event has completed, so the
callback runs on the computing thread; events are pooled and at most 1024
records wait.

Host spans: cuda.wait around synchronize, cuda.download and cuda.upload
around blocking tensor copies (the path resident-prefix checkpoints take),
cuda.graph_capture around capturing and instantiating or updating a CUDA
graph, and cuda.graph_ops once per graph size. HIP and MUSA builds report
the same under rocm and musa; their vendor headers map the event calls
used.

With no command timing callback installed a compute costs one atomic load.
test-skippy-tracing-cuda checks three tagged computes on a real device.
